<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Abu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Abu is the highest peak of the Aravalli Range, in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> border. It is famous for the Dilwara temples, marble Jain temples built under the Chaulukya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghela</a> rulers of Gujarat in the 11th to 13th centuries. Legend made it the site of the sacrificial fire from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnikula">Agnikula</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans, including the Paramaras, were born.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-abu.jpg" alt="Mount Abu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas Kleemann · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Abu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
